    "baseRefName": "main",
    "body": "## Why this PR exists\n\nPRs 152, 153, and 154 were marked merged, but their bases were feature branches rather than `main`. Their workshop rebase, bridge notes, and article rewrite therefore remained on the stack branches. Current `main` contains the containment and Greenfield ontology imports, but not the rest of the refoundation.\n\nThis PR lands the stranded work on current `main` and applies the precision fixes identified during review.\n\n## Lands on `main`\n\n- the re-based agentic-system-learning workshop and conceptual spine;\n- six durable bridge notes from agentic substrate through factory-level continual learning to theory mediation;\n- the research-program article restructured so theory mediation is a candidate mechanism rather than an architectural premise.\n\n## Greenfield cleanup\n\n- preserve the 2003 configured-IDE formulation separately from the mature 2007 development-and-runtime-environment definition;\n- reserve **factory specialization** for Greenfield's operation on reusable viewpoints, artifacts, activities, and assets;\n- use **acquisition of family-specific production knowledge** for the stronger learning question;\n- distinguish candidate factory-development artifacts from installed revisions;\n- restore the member-specific configured-schema caveat;\n- keep prompts and evaluators in the later agentic mapping rather than the historical ontology;\n- remove the computational-closure leak from the construction/acquisition comparison;\n- update Greenfield, Tool Factory, and MDSoFa source-to-note provenance.\n\nThe former construction note path remains as a compatibility pointer so existing links do not break.\n\n## Boundaries preserved\n\n- a fixed universal substrate remains possible in principle;\n- task families do not automatically define software product families;\n- factory construction from supplied knowledge is not acquisition of that knowledge;\n- factory learning does not imply improvement, reflection, closure, autonomy, breadth, or compounding;\n- theory mediation remains a comparative hypothesis against trial and error, trajectories, program search, learned policies, optimization, and mixtures.\n",
    "closedAt": "2026-08-31T13:40:02Z",
